  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLingering wildfire smoke across the region prompted \na Code Orange Air Quality Alert (issued by Pennsylvania and \nWest Virginia's Dept. of Environmental Protection). Much of the \neffects across the region were from lingering smoke trapped \nunderneath the mornings SFC inversion but as that has broken and\nmixed out, air quality has been on the rise and is back into \nModerate across the Pittsburgh Metro. Smoke shifts north and \neast through the day improving air quality as it does.\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA strong low-pressure system tracking across southern Ontario \nunzips a warm sector across the region on Tuesday. Upper level \nsupport is provided by an uncharacteristically strong (for this \ntime of year) jet left exit region. Warm and humid air (low 70s \ndew points) fuel instability estimates that range from 1500 J/kg\non the low end to reasonably high end expectations near 2500 \nJ/kg. This in combination with strong wind shear (35-45 kts of \n0-6km shear) will create a favorable environment for organized \nthunderstorms. \n\nModeling favors early morning convection well to our west \nalthough a few pre-dawn storms can't be ruled out in central and\neastern Ohio. Some uncertainty remains in the initial \nconvection due to how the most recent run of models handle a \ndecaying MCS near Cincinnati tomorrow morning. Despite this, the\nmost likely timing of severe weather remains between 12-8pm. \nModels suggest activity begins late morning or midday along a \nlake boundary closer to CLE and advances ESE into our area. \nStorms are then increasingly favored further south and east \nacross Pittsburgh and the I-70 corridor into the afternoon \nbefore convective coverage begins to wane after sunset. \n\nThe primary threat from storms tomorrow remains damaging wind \ngusts along some kind of developing squall line although \ntornadoes and large hail remain possible in isolated supercells \nthat could develop out in front of the main line. The Storm \nPrediction Center has maintained their Enhanced Risk (level 3 \nout of 5) across the region for tomorrow. \n\nAnother threat from this activity remains heavy rain. PWATs are\nexpected to be above the 95th percentile and could reach up \ntowards the daily maximum values (1.96 inches). This accompanied\nwith a relatively high freezing level will allow for efficient \nwarm rain processes in a very moist environment. Storms are \nexpected to be rather progressive, which would favor flash flood\nconcerns requiring training or multiple rounds of rain in the \nsame area. Modeling seems to supply this in aggressive estimates\nhaving 3.00-4.00 inches of rain in places mainly along the I-70\ncorridor. This southern focus could potentially keep the \nheaviest rain out of areas that have recently seen it but brings\nit much closer to our urban centers where flood risk is \ninherently higher. Additional confidence in the areal location \nof these higher rainfall totals is needed before the issuance of\nany Flood Watch. Hourly rates in the heaviest thunderstorms \nlook like they could top out near 2.00 inches/hr. The Weather \nPrediction Center has held their Slight Risk of excessive \nrainfall (level 2 out of 5). \n\nExpect lingering showers and non-severe thunderstorms into \nearly Wednesday before a cold front tracks through the region \nafter sunrise. \n\nThe potential for precipitation remain lows through the \nremainder of the work week and into the weekend before showers \nand storms again look possible on Sunday.",
